 ## Architecture
 
-Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed regardless of the lookup backend. The certificates node uses only this interface.
+Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed. The certificates node uses only this interface.
 
-Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is not selected yet. Candidates are a local embedded SQLite index, an on-site HTTPS directory, and a managed search service. No certificate behavior or interface change is part of that selection.
+Snapshot validation, index preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. Lookup is served from a local embedded SQLite index file built on the kiosk from the snapshot; no part of lookup or import touches the network.
 
 ## Snapshot Import
 
-Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Prepare the new snapshot without exposing partially imported records. Activate it only after all validation succeeds. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot active and reports a useful error.
+1. Verify the signature using a bundled public key.
+2. Reject unsupported schema versions.
+3. Build a new SQLite index file in a staging location from the snapshot's records, enforcing serial-number uniqueness while building.
+4. Activate the staged index only after every validation and build step has succeeded, by a single atomic switch of which index file is active.
+
+Lookups continue against the previously active index until the switch, and the staged index is never visible to lookups. An import failure at any step discards the staged index, leaves the previous snapshot active, and reports a useful error.
 
 ## Lookup Behavior
 
-Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. The concrete index and query path remain open pending the user's choice.
+Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Query the active index by normalized serial number. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Lookup backend | Local embedded SQLite index, built on the kiosk at import | On-site HTTPS directory; managed search service | Lookup must complete from files on the kiosk with no runtime network access (HLD tenets), which rules out both networked backends. SQLite needs no service to run and gives predictable lookups. The cost is that each kiosk validates and rebuilds its own index when a snapshot arrives, contained within this node. |
+| Index activation | Build in staging, then atomically switch the active index | Modify the active index in place | Satisfies the HLD tenet that a failed import leaves the last valid snapshot available, and keeps partial imports invisible to lookups. Costs disk space for two index files during import. |
